1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,169
Salt falls on a gas flame,
and the flame flashes yellow.

2
00:00:04,169 --> 00:00:06,170
Fireworks burst red and green.

3
00:00:06,170 --> 00:00:08,425
Street lamps glow orange at night.

4
00:00:08,825 --> 00:00:10,272
Three different glows.

5
00:00:10,272 --> 00:00:13,184
Each colour is a signature,
written by atoms.

6
00:00:13,634 --> 00:00:15,450
Diya sees it in her kitchen.

7
00:00:15,450 --> 00:00:19,362
A pinch of salt hits the blue
flame, and it turns bright yellow.

8
00:00:19,712 --> 00:00:22,731
That yellow comes from
sodium atoms in the salt.

9
00:00:22,731 --> 00:00:25,210
Every element glows in its own colours.

10
00:00:25,710 --> 00:00:29,341
By the end, you will read
hydrogen's colours like a barcode.

11
00:00:29,991 --> 00:00:31,453
So what is a spectrum?

12
00:00:31,803 --> 00:00:36,306
White light through a prism spreads
into colours, violet to red, with no

13
00:00:36,306 --> 00:00:37,206
gaps.

14
00:00:36,851 --> 00:00:38,339
A continuous spectrum.

15
00:00:38,689 --> 00:00:43,190
Now heat a gas of one element until
it glows, and pass its light through

16
00:00:43,190 --> 00:00:44,090
the prism.

17
00:00:44,383 --> 00:00:46,913
You get only a few sharp bright lines.

18
00:00:46,913 --> 00:00:49,126
An emission, or line, spectrum.

19
00:00:49,476 --> 00:00:53,830
Shine white light through the cool
gas, and those same lines go dark.

20
00:00:53,830 --> 00:00:55,332
An absorption spectrum.

21
00:00:55,982 --> 00:00:59,165
Each element has its own
lines, like a fingerprint.

22
00:00:59,165 --> 00:01:02,478
Rubidium, caesium and gallium
were discovered this way.

23
00:01:03,128 --> 00:01:05,605
Now hydrogen, on our molecule bench.

24
00:01:05,605 --> 00:01:09,405
A tube of hydrogen glows when
electricity passes through it.

25
00:01:09,755 --> 00:01:13,416
Through the prism, its visible
light splits into four lines.

26
00:01:13,416 --> 00:01:16,076
Red, blue green, blue violet and violet.

27
00:01:16,476 --> 00:01:18,723
Each sits at an exact wavelength.

28
00:01:18,723 --> 00:01:23,934
About six fifty six, four eighty six,
four thirty four and four ten nanometres.

29
00:01:24,334 --> 00:01:25,577
Why only these?

30
00:01:25,577 --> 00:01:27,199
Look inside the atom.

31
00:01:27,199 --> 00:01:31,160
The electron takes in energy
and jumps to a higher orbit.

32
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:33,069
Then it falls back.

33
00:01:33,069 --> 00:01:36,580
The energy it loses leaves
as one photon of light.

34
00:01:36,980 --> 00:01:39,220
Three to two gives the red line.

35
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:41,046
Four to two, blue green.

36
00:01:41,046 --> 00:01:42,921
Five to two, blue violet.

37
00:01:42,921 --> 00:01:44,440
Six to two, violet.

38
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:47,233
All end on orbit two: the Balmer series.

39
00:01:47,883 --> 00:01:50,986
Falls that end on orbit
one have bigger gaps.

40
00:01:50,986 --> 00:01:54,018
Their light is ultraviolet,
invisible to us.

41
00:01:54,018 --> 00:01:55,855
This is the Lyman series.

42
00:01:56,255 --> 00:02:00,477
Falls that end on orbit three have
smaller gaps, in the infrared.

43
00:02:00,477 --> 00:02:01,826
The Paschen series.

44
00:02:02,226 --> 00:02:05,751
Brackett ends on orbit
four, Pfund on orbit five.

45
00:02:05,751 --> 00:02:06,880
Both infrared.

46
00:02:07,530 --> 00:02:08,645
Side by side.

47
00:02:08,645 --> 00:02:12,091
Lyman falls end on orbit
one, in the ultraviolet.

48
00:02:12,091 --> 00:02:15,382
Balmer falls end on orbit
two, in the visible.

49
00:02:15,782 --> 00:02:16,990
Pause and think.

50
00:02:16,990 --> 00:02:21,319
Which carries more energy, any
Lyman photon, or any Balmer photon?

51
00:02:24,519 --> 00:02:25,817
Any Lyman photon.

52
00:02:25,817 --> 00:02:30,674
The smallest Lyman gap, two to one,
is bigger than even the largest Balmer

53
00:02:30,674 --> 00:02:31,574
gap.

54
00:02:31,841 --> 00:02:34,206
Now the formula, one step at a time.

55
00:02:34,556 --> 00:02:35,456
Step one.

56
00:02:35,349 --> 00:02:39,574
In eighteen eighty five, Balmer found
the pattern in the visible lines

57
00:02:39,574 --> 00:02:40,753
using wavenumber.

58
00:02:40,753 --> 00:02:43,924
Wavenumber, nu bar, is
one over the wavelength.

59
00:02:44,674 --> 00:02:45,577
Step two.

60
00:02:45,577 --> 00:02:48,407
Rydberg made it work for every series.

61
00:02:48,407 --> 00:02:53,747
Nu bar equals one zero nine six seven
seven, times one over n one squared,

62
00:02:53,747 --> 00:02:57,138
minus one over n two
squared, per centimetre.

63
00:02:57,888 --> 00:02:58,813
Step three.

64
00:02:58,813 --> 00:03:03,749
n one is the orbit the electron falls
to, n two the orbit it falls from.

65
00:03:03,749 --> 00:03:05,669
So n two is always bigger.

66
00:03:06,419 --> 00:03:07,319
Step four.

67
00:03:07,228 --> 00:03:10,351
Set n one to two, and
you get the Balmer lines.

68
00:03:10,351 --> 00:03:11,932
Set it to one, Lyman.

69
00:03:11,932 --> 00:03:13,066
Three, Paschen.

70
00:03:13,816 --> 00:03:14,934
Remember this.

71
00:03:14,934 --> 00:03:19,877
Every line in a spectrum is one electron
falling between two fixed orbits.

72
00:03:20,577 --> 00:03:22,312
Now, one worked example.

73
00:03:22,612 --> 00:03:26,720
Find the wavelength of the red line,
the fall from orbit three to orbit

74
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:27,620
two.

75
00:03:27,682 --> 00:03:29,944
n one is two, n two is three.

76
00:03:29,944 --> 00:03:34,082
One over four, minus one over
nine, is five over thirty six.

77
00:03:34,832 --> 00:03:37,377
Times one zero nine six seven seven.

78
00:03:37,377 --> 00:03:42,035
Nu bar is fifteen thousand two hundred
and thirty three per centimetre.

79
00:03:42,785 --> 00:03:47,963
Wavelength is one over nu bar, six
point five six five times ten to the

80
00:03:47,963 --> 00:03:49,742
minus five centimetres.

81
00:03:50,492 --> 00:03:53,273
About six hundred and
fifty six nanometres.

82
00:03:53,273 --> 00:03:55,452
Red, just as the bench showed.

83
00:03:56,102 --> 00:03:57,565
Now, the exam trap.

84
00:03:57,915 --> 00:04:02,464
Some students put the higher orbit as
n one, and get a negative wavenumber.

85
00:04:02,864 --> 00:04:06,641
n one is always the lower
orbit, where the electron lands.

86
00:04:06,641 --> 00:04:08,437
n two is where it starts.

87
00:04:09,087 --> 00:04:10,482
Where is this asked?

88
00:04:10,482 --> 00:04:14,686
As numericals on the wavelength of
a line, and on which series lies in

89
00:04:14,686 --> 00:04:15,641
which region.

90
00:04:16,241 --> 00:04:17,442
Exam level now.

91
00:04:17,442 --> 00:04:21,621
What is the shortest wavelength
in the Lyman series of hydrogen?

92
00:04:21,621 --> 00:04:22,872
Pause and solve.

93
00:04:28,272 --> 00:04:29,614
The answer is C.

94
00:04:29,614 --> 00:04:32,223
About ninety one point two nanometres.

95
00:04:32,723 --> 00:04:35,423
Shortest wavelength means the biggest gap.

96
00:04:35,423 --> 00:04:39,329
That is a fall from n equals
infinity, down to orbit one.

97
00:04:39,829 --> 00:04:41,887
One over infinity is zero.

98
00:04:41,887 --> 00:04:46,193
So nu bar is one zero nine six
seven seven per centimetre.

99
00:04:46,693 --> 00:04:49,310
That is ninety one point two nanometres.

100
00:04:49,310 --> 00:04:51,192
A is the two to one line.

101
00:04:51,192 --> 00:04:53,574
D is the end of the Balmer series.

102
00:04:54,174 --> 00:04:55,633
Here is your recap.

103
00:04:55,633 --> 00:04:57,133
Screenshot this card.

104
00:05:00,333 --> 00:05:03,331
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

105
00:05:03,731 --> 00:05:04,631
One.

106
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:08,081
How is a line spectrum different
from a continuous spectrum?

107
00:05:08,881 --> 00:05:09,781
Two.

108
00:05:09,402 --> 00:05:14,131
Which hydrogen series is visible,
and on which orbit do its falls end?

109
00:05:14,931 --> 00:05:15,831
Three.

110
00:05:15,554 --> 00:05:18,616
Why must n two always
be bigger than n one?

111
00:05:19,516 --> 00:05:23,669
Next time: is light a wave,
or a stream of tiny packets?

112
00:05:23,669 --> 00:05:28,115
We find out in electromagnetic
radiation and Planck's quantum theory.
